<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Story of Wenamun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1075 BCE</span> · Also known as Report of Wenamun, Tale of Wenamun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Story of Wenamun is an ancient Egyptian text, preserved on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> found at el-Hiba and now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, describing the journey of Wenamun, an official of the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> to buy cedar for the god's sacred barque. Set at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twentieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twentieth Dynasty</a>, it shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s weakened standing abroad, as King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakar-baal">Zakar-Baal</a> of Byblos demands payment rather than tribute.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/story-of-wenamun.jpg" alt="Story of Wenamun" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ancient Egyptian Writer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wenamun-papyrus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
